# MCP tool stress / performance

When creating or updating a plan (Plan mode / CreatePlan), or when implementing under `mcp-server/` or `scripts/stress/`, that **adds, removes, renames, or meaningfully changes** an MCP tool (handler, args, payload shape, hot path) or the stress harness — include a **Stress / performance** section.

If the change is unrelated (typo, pure CSS, dependency bump, changelog-only, explain copy with no size/I/O change), mark the section **N/A** with a one-line reason. Do not invent fake stress work; do not omit the section when MCP tools *are* in scope.

Harness: `npm run test:stress` → [`scripts/stress/scenarios.ts`](scripts/stress/scenarios.ts) (+ optional [`scripts/stress/budgets.ts`](scripts/stress/budgets.ts) `byId`) → `artifacts/stress-report.{json,md,html}`.

Not every tool needs a new scenario. Decide explicitly: **add**, **covered**, or **skip**.

## Required decision (do not skip when in scope)

For each manipulated tool, record briefly:

1. **Frequency** — How often agents call this (bootstrap / every session, common read, rare mutate, docs-only)
2. **Impact** — Latency, payload/token size, disk/DB/network, concurrency risk under burst
3. **Decision** — one of:
   - **Add / extend scenario** — new or updated row in `SCENARIOS` (and plain-English `about` for the HTML report)
   - **Covered already** — name the existing scenario `id`(s); no harness change
   - **Skip** — one-line reason (rare, mutate-only, tiny control, covered by sibling tool)

Stress stays in plan + implement wrap-up (+ `scenarios.ts` when adding). Do **not** require stress decisions in `agent-changelog.yml`.

## When to add or extend a scenario

Prefer adding (or tightening args / `phase: "both"` / heavy variant) when **any** of:

- New **high-frequency read** (list/get used in normal agent loops)
- Payload can grow large (full pages, catalogs, `include_*` flags, docs/skills)
- New mode/arg that changes cost (pagination, wide date windows, `limit`, search-engines, skill content)
- Hot path shares locks/caches with other tools (risk under concurrent burst)
- Existing scenario no longer matches the tool contract (rename, required args, default size)

## When skip is OK

- Mutating tools the harness intentionally does **not** call (stress is read-only)
- Ultra-rare admin/debug tools with tiny fixed payloads
- Pure response-envelope / `warnings` / `next_actions` changes with no I/O or size change
- Thin wrappers already represented by a heavier sibling scenario

## How to add a scenario (when decision = add)

1. Append a row to `SCENARIOS` in [`scripts/stress/scenarios.ts`](scripts/stress/scenarios.ts) (`id`, `tool`, plain-English `about`, `buildArgs` / `args`, `skipIf` if needed).
2. Use `phase: "both"` only for tools that should also appear in the concurrent burst.
3. Use `class: "docs"` for large bootstrap/explain-style payloads.
4. Override `budgets.byId[id]` only when the default band is wrong — keep bands loose until you have a baseline.
5. Re-run `npm run test:stress` (or `--skip-pull` if content is fresh) and confirm the new row appears in the HTML report with sane p95 / tokens.

## Anti-patterns

| Anti-pattern | Do instead |
|---|---|
| Ship a new list/get tool with no stress thought | Decide add / covered / skip explicitly |
| Duplicate scenarios for every arg combo | One representative + optional heavy/light contrast |
| Skip “because stress is hard” on hot reads | Prefer a small sequential scenario over nothing |
| Add mutate/write scenarios to the harness | Keep stress read-only; document skip |
| Change tool args but leave stale `buildArgs` | Update or retire the scenario in the same change |
| Invent stress work on typo/CSS plans | Mark Stress / performance **N/A** |

## Plan section shape

In the plan’s **Stress / performance** section:

- **Tools touched:** …
- **Per tool:** frequency · impact · decision (`add` | `covered:<id>` | `skip:reason`)
- **Harness edits:** none | `scenarios.ts` (± `budgets.ts`)
